## Containers And Storage

### ἀγγεῖον / ἀγγεῖον πλατύστομον
<a id="term-aggeion-aggeion-platystomon"></a>

Meaning: General vessel; wide-mouthed version especially useful for transfers, infusions, and vapor-contact setups.

Why it matters: Shows that vessel shape mattered to the process, not just the presence of a container.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-aggeion-aggeion-platystomon)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-aggeion-aggeion-platystomon)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Ancient perfume texts distinguish ordinary vessels from wide-mouthed working vessels.
- Some stages required an open, accessible mouth for straining, layering, or suspended aromatics.

Do not infer:
- That one standard vessel shape served every stage.

### κρατήρ
<a id="term-krater"></a>

Meaning: Large bowl or vat for receiving and storing prepared liquid.

Why it matters: Indicates bulk handling and intermediate storage, not only tiny luxury flasks.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-krater)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-krater)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Pressed perfume could be moved into a larger receiving vessel.
- Honey-coated vessels appear as a practical preservation measure in recipe context.

### λουτήρ / λουτηρίδιον
<a id="term-loyter-loyteridion"></a>

Meaning: Broad shallow basin; diminutive for smaller re-maceration basin.

Why it matters: One of the clearest indicators that delicate flowers were steeped in a shape chosen to protect them.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-loyter-loyteridion)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-loyter-loyteridion)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Flower-oil production used broad, shallow basins.
- The same material could be re-soaked in a smaller basin for another pressing cycle.

Do not infer:
- That `louter` in this dossier means bathing equipment.

### βῖκος / βικίον
<a id="term-bikos-bikion"></a>

Meaning: Jar used in steeping, storage, and sealed handling; often glass in later medical and technical contexts.

Why it matters: Central vessel for sun-steeping, storage, and controlled closure.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-bikos-bikion)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-bikos-bikion)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Perfume work used tightly bound jars during maceration.
- Glass jars appear as preferred storage for finished aromatic products.

Do not infer:
- That every `bikos` reference is perfumery; the term also has non-vessel senses outside the core evidence.

### ἀγγεῖον ὑάλινον / glass jars
<a id="term-aggeion-hyalinon-glass-jars"></a>

Meaning: Glass storage vessel for refined or finished perfume.

Why it matters: High-yield post angle on scent preservation and material choice.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-aggeion-hyalinon-glass-jars)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-aggeion-hyalinon-glass-jars)
Citations: 0

Safe claims:
- Finished oils were sometimes transferred into glass vessels.
- Storage material was treated as technically meaningful.

### μυροθήκη
<a id="term-myrotheke"></a>

Meaning: Either the perfume container itself or the case holding perfume flasks.

Why it matters: Bridges production and retail presentation.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-myrotheke)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-myrotheke)
